# =====================================================================
# base64_6x.fdc  --  Synplify Pro constraints for the 6x turbo build
#
# WHAT CHANGED vs base64.fdc, and why
#
#   The old file did:
#       create_clock -name clk_7m ... -period 141.0
#       set_clock_groups -asynchronous -group clk_12x -group clk_7m
#
#   You objected to that line. You were right, but for a better reason
#   than "the 570 is phase aligned": in this design clk_7m is not a clock
#   at all. Nothing is clocked by it. It is sampled as DATA by a 2FF
#   synchroniser running on clk_12x, and every "7M domain" flop is a
#   clk_12x flop with a clock enable. There is exactly one clock in the
#   design. So clk_7m must not be declared with create_clock, and once it
#   is not declared there is no second group to declare asynchronous --
#   the set_clock_groups line disappears on its own.
#
#   Declaring it as a clock was actively harmful: it made Synplify build a
#   CDC model for a crossing that does not exist, and the -asynchronous
#   escape hatch then silently disabled timing analysis on paths that DO
#   need checking (the bridge's pin registers).
# =====================================================================

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. The one real clock
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Constrain to the NTSC period (the faster of the two) so a single
# bitstream is valid on both machines:
#     PAL   7.09379 MHz x 12 = 85.126 MHz -> 11.747 ns
#     NTSC  7.15909 MHz x 12 = 85.909 MHz -> 11.640 ns
create_clock -name clk_12x -period 11.640 [get_ports clk_12x]

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. clk_7m is DATA, not a clock
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# It feeds only the first stage of a synchroniser, which is allowed to go
# metastable by construction. False-path it so the tool does not try to
# close a setup arc on an input with no defined phase relationship.
#
# IMPORTANT -- do not "improve" this by adding a create_clock back. What
# protects the PHASE_OFS alignment is not this constraint, it is the
# negedge capture in section 2 of the RTL: the sample point sits 5.82 ns
# away from the clk_12x rising edge, so several ns of PVT movement on the
# input path cannot change WHICH master clock captures the 7M edge. That
# is a design property, not a timing-closure property.
set_false_path -from [get_ports clk_7m]

# Same argument for every genuinely asynchronous motherboard input: all
# of them land in a 2FF synchroniser (RTL section 2).
set_false_path -from [get_ports {cpu_dtack_n cpu_vpa_n cpu_berr_n}]
set_false_path -from [get_ports {cpu_br_n cpu_bgack_n cpu_reset_n cpu_halt_n}]
set_false_path -from [get_ports {cpu_ipl_n[*] cpu_cfgin_n}]

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. Bus pins -- the constraint the old file was missing entirely
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# The bridge drives these from dedicated pin registers. Pack them into
# the IOBs: that is what keeps skew between address bits (and between
# address and AS) down to package/route mismatch instead of fabric
# routing spread. At 7 MHz the absolute delay is irrelevant -- the SKEW
# is what Gary and the RAM see.
define_attribute {i:p_a[*]}    {syn_useioff} {1}
define_attribute {i:p_d[*]}    {syn_useioff} {1}
define_attribute {i:p_fc[*]}   {syn_useioff} {1}
define_attribute {i:p_rw}      {syn_useioff} {1}
define_attribute {i:p_as_n}    {syn_useioff} {1}
define_attribute {i:p_uds_n}   {syn_useioff} {1}
define_attribute {i:p_lds_n}   {syn_useioff} {1}
define_attribute {i:p_vma_n}   {syn_useioff} {1}
# p_e omitted on purpose: Synplify reported "Cannot apply constraint
# syn_useioff to p_e" because it had already absorbed the register into
# the pad. cpu_e arrives in 3.958 ns, so it is already packed.

# DO NOT add set_output_delay here. The first build did, and it was wrong:
#
#   set_output_delay -clock clk_12x -max 6.0 [get_ports {cpu_a[*] ...}]
#
# That says "the destination samples this relative to clk_12x with 6 ns of
# setup". Gary does not sample on clk_12x. The constraint is fiction, and
# it made the tool report 336 setup errors and an Fmax of 79.4 MHz on a
# design whose logic actually closes 86.9 MHz. Worse, it BURIED the one
# number that matters -- fx68k's real slack -- under 47 output ports that
# were never going to meet it.
#
# For the record, the measured arrival times with that constraint in place:
#
#   cpu_a[*], cpu_as_n, cpu_fc[*], strobes   6.190 ns
#   cpu_d[*]                                 6.592 ns
#   cpu_e                                    3.958 ns  (IOB packed, no OE)
#
# Every one of those is registered on the same clk_12x edge, so the SKEW is
# 0 ns across the address bus, 0 ns between address and AS, and 0.4 ns
# between address and data. On a bus whose period is 141 ns and where AS
# falls a deliberate 70 ns after the address is valid, that is not a
# timing problem in any sense that reaches the motherboard.
#
# The ~2.2 ns that cpu_a costs over cpu_e is the tri-state enable path
# (two LUTs) plus the BB buffer with SLEWRATE=SLOW from base64.lpf. The
# slow slew rate is deliberate -- it is there to keep the edges civil on a
# long unterminated backplane -- and trading it for timing margin you do
# not need would be a bad trade.
#
# If you ever do want a real output constraint, the honest form is a
# max-delay to the pin, not a setup relative to a clock the far end has
# never heard of:
#
#   set_max_delay 9.0 -to [get_ports {cpu_a[*] cpu_d[*] cpu_as_n}]

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. fx68k -- READ THIS BEFORE TOUCHING IT
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Every fx68k flop is clocked by clk_12x and gated by enPhi1 or enPhi2.
# STA therefore demands that EVERY path close in one master clock period
# (11.64 ns) unless you tell it otherwise. The real settling time
# available depends on which enable is at each end:
#
#   at CLK_MULT=12, CPU_MULT=6  (MC4 = 2 master clocks per CPU period)
#     phi1 -> phi2 :  1 master clock  = 11.64 ns   <-- CANNOT be relaxed
#     phi2 -> phi1 :  1 master clock  = 11.64 ns   <-- CANNOT be relaxed
#     phi1 -> phi1 :  2 master clocks = 23.28 ns   <-- multicycle 2
#     phi2 -> phi2 :  2 master clocks = 23.28 ns   <-- multicycle 2
#
# So the hard requirement is that fx68k's phi1->phi2 logic closes at
# 85.9 MHz. No constraint can buy you out of that, and neither can a
# faster master clock (see DESIGN_NOTES_6x.md section "Why not 24x").
#
# UPDATE -- the first build settled this. Only TWO paths fail, by 0.020 ns
# and 0.001 ns, and both are legitimately multicycle. From your fork:
#
#   enT1 = enPhi1 & tState[T4] & ~wClk
#   enT2 = enPhi2 & tState[T1]
#   enT3 = enPhi1 & tState[T2]
#   enT4 = enPhi2 & (tState[T0] | tState[T3])
#
# so the four T slots land phi1,phi2,phi1,phi2 and a full microcycle is
# 4 master clocks at CPU_MULT=6. The two failing paths are:
#
#   rCcrCore_t3 (enT3) -> rPswCcr_t3 (enT3)   T3->T3, a whole microcycle
#                                             = 4 master clocks = 46.6 ns
#   oper        (enT4) -> rPswCcr_t3 (enT3)   T4->T3, three T slots
#                                             = 3 master clocks = 34.9 ns
#
# Both are being held to 11.64 ns, i.e. over-constrained by 3-4x. The
# 0.020 ns "failure" is meaningless. These two are safe to relax, and
# -setup 2 is deliberately conservative against a real 3-4.

set_multicycle_path -setup 2 -hold 1 \
    -from [get_cells {cpu/excUnit/U_fx68kAlu/rCcrCore_t3*}] \
    -to   [get_cells {cpu/excUnit/U_fx68kAlu/rPswCcr_t3*}]

set_multicycle_path -setup 2 -hold 1 \
    -from [get_cells {cpu/excUnit/U_fx68kAlu/oper*}] \
    -to   [get_cells {cpu/excUnit/U_fx68kAlu/rPswCcr_t3*}]

# NOTE the -hold 1 companion on each. A -setup N without a matching
# -hold N-1 moves the hold check to the wrong edge and produces hold
# violations that look like a routing problem and are not. Hold currently
# passes with 0 errors -- check it still does after adding these.
#
# Do NOT generalise these to a blanket cpu/* form. T1->T2, T2->T3,
# T3->T4 and T4->T1 really do have only one master clock, and relaxing
# them would give you a build that passes STA and fails on hardware.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# 5. Bridge <-> core
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# These are ordinary single-clock paths and must be analysed normally.
# Listed here only to make it explicit that they are NOT false paths --
# the old -asynchronous group would have swallowed them.
#   bridge FSM  -> pin registers        : real, constrained in section 3
#   sync flops  -> bridge FSM           : real
#   bridge      -> core_dtack_lo/rd_data: real, and the tightest of the
#                                         three because of ACK_LEAD
